<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercisegroup xml:id="exercise-separation-axioms" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Separation axioms
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Determine which separation axioms are satisfied by the given topological space.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is an indiscrete topological space <m> \of{ X , \bot_{ X } } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        If <m> X </m> has at most one element, then the indiscrete topological space <m> \of{ X , \bot_{ X } } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m>. However, if <m> X </m> has at least two elements, then the indiscrete topological space <m> \of{ X , \bot_{ X } } </m> is neither <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, nor <m> T_{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is discrete topological space <m> \of{ X , \top_{ X } } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The discrete topological space <m> \of{ X , \top_{ X } } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Is Euclidean space <m> \of{ \R^{ n } , \tau_{ d } } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        Euclidean space <m> \of{ \R^{ n } , \tau_{ d } } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \tau </m> be the cofinite topology on a set <m> X </m>. Is the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m> if <m> X </m> is finite. However, if <m> X </m> is infinite, then the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is only <m> T_{ 0 } </m> and <m> T_{ 1 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \tau </m> be the cocountable topology on a set <m> X </m>. Is the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m> if <m> X </m> is countable. However, if <m> X </m> is uncountable, then the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is only <m> T_{ 0 } </m> and <m> T_{ 1 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \tau </m> be a particular point topology on a set <m> X </m>. Is the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m> if <m> X </m> has at most one element. However, if <m> X </m> has at least two elements, then the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is only <m> T_{ 0 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  
  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m> \tau </m> be an excluded point topology on a set <m> X </m>. Is the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> <m> T_{ 0 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 1 } </m>? Is it <m> T_{ 2 } </m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <answer>
      <p>
        The topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is <m> T_{ 0 } </m>, <m> T_{ 1 } </m>, and <m> T_{ 2 } </m> if <m> X </m> has at most one element. However, if <m> X </m> has at least two elements, then the topological space <m> \of{ X , \tau } </m> is only <m> T_{ 0 } </m>.
      </p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</exercisegroup>
